  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "As US and China form AI blocs, countries increasingly torn between them",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/technology/artificial-intelligence/as-us-and-china-form-ai-blocs-countries-increasingly-torn-between-them-126102500177_1.html",
      "description": "With the two leading AI powers, the United States and China, forming their own AI blocs, other countries are maneuvering to harness the strengths of both groupings and avoid losing out.\nAs the rivalry intensifies, experts say the window for countries to be able to choose freely between the two AI superpowers may be narrowing, forcing them to at one point pick between the US-led Pax Silica and the China-inspired World AI Cooperation Organisation.\nThat could put many countries in a dilemma: Do they go with the US' most advanced chips and AI technologies, or join China's more affordable, open-source AI systems?\nKazakhstan, a Central Asia nation of about 21 million people, is a rare example of a country that has sought to bridge that gap.\nJust three weeks after its AI minister Zhaslan Madiyev visited Washington in June to join the US group working to strengthen AI supply chains, he headed to Shanghai for a signing ceremony making the Central Asian country a founding member of China's .",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-25T07:04:35"
    },
    {
      "title": "r/opensource just banned AI, non-English speakers, and more",
      "link": "https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1018217/reddit-opensource-ai-ban",
      "description": "The mods at r/opensource posted new submission rules that, among other things, banned projects that use AI. The post made Saturday evening opens by saying, \"If your project uses AI, is made by AI, or is intended for AI, it's not welcome on r/opensource. AI, as it exists today, is incongruent to this Open Source [&#8230;]",
      "source_name": "The Verge All Posts",
      "published": "2026-10-25T13:25:32"
    }
